<div align="center">

# Cubeless UnrealMCP for Unreal Engine 5.6.1

An editor-authoring MCP bridge with deep Blueprint, Niagara, PCG, Control Rig,
Chaos, AI/BT/EQS, GAS, Networking, Audio/MetaSound, StateTree, material, and
validation tooling.

![Unreal Engine](https://img.shields.io/badge/Unreal%20Engine-5.6.1-orange)
![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.11-yellow)
![MCP transport](https://img.shields.io/badge/MCP-stdio-blue)

</div>

This repository is the Cubeless Unreal Engine 5.6.1 integration fork of
[`chongdashu/unreal-mcp`](https://github.com/chongdashu/unreal-mcp). It connects
an MCP client to a running Unreal Editor through an external Python MCP server
and an editor-only C++ bridge plugin.

The current integration is validated for **Unreal Engine 5.6.1 on Windows**.
It is not Epic's experimental Unreal MCP implementation introduced in Unreal
Engine 5.8, and it does not currently wrap the UE 5.8 Toolset Registry.

## What is included

- 429 registered tool contracts across 25 toolsets.
- Compact discovery through `list_toolsets`, `describe_toolset`, and
  `call_tool` instead of advertising every schema at startup.
- Deep Blueprint graph, Niagara stack/graph, PCG, Control Rig, Chaos,
  AI/BT/EQS, GAS, Networking, Audio/MetaSound, StateTree, material, Sequencer,
  data, landscape, skeletal, UMG, and editor operations.
- Runtime policy metadata for read, write, destructive, and lifecycle tools.
- Confirmation gates for high-risk dispatches and structured mutation results.
- Transactional `execute_batch` support for up to 100 bridge commands in one
  editor transaction.
- Guards intended to keep authored project content independent from the
  UnrealMCP plugin after authoring is complete.

The repository also contains `MCPGameProject`, the UE 5.6 integration and test
project used to build and validate the plugin. It is not required in a game
project that already has the matching UnrealMCP distribution installed.

## Architecture

```text
MCP client
  -> stdio
Python/unreal_mcp_server.py (FastMCP, external Python 3.11)
  -> TCP 127.0.0.1:55561
UnrealMCP editor plugin
  -> Unreal Editor subsystems and game-thread command handlers
```

The Unreal bridge is loopback-only and listens on port `55561` by default.
Each bridge command uses a bounded request/response exchange; the external MCP
server reconnects for each command.

## Requirements

- Unreal Engine **5.6.1**. Other engine versions are not claimed by this
  checkout.
- A matching UE 5.6.1 UnrealMCP plugin build.
- Python **3.11** for the external MCP server.
- [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) for the locked Python environment.
- An MCP client that can launch a stdio server.
- Visual Studio with the C++ workload only when building the plugin from
  source. A matching prebuilt plugin does not require a local plugin build.

## Quick start

### 1. Install and enable the plugin

Install the base plugin and any required optional companion folders as siblings
under the target project's `Plugins` directory (do not nest the companions
inside `UnrealMCP`):

```text
<YourProject>/Plugins/
  UnrealMCP/                 required base editor bridge
  UnrealMCPStateTree/        optional StateTree authoring provider
  UnrealMCPMetaSound/        optional MetaSound authoring provider
```

Enable **UnrealMCP** and its required editor plugins, then restart Unreal
Editor. Install and enable an optional companion only for the domain that needs
it:

- GAS: enable the UE 5.6 `GameplayAbilities` plugin; no companion is required.
- MetaSound: enable UE 5.6 `Metasound` and `UnrealMCPMetaSound`.
- StateTree: enable UE 5.6 `StateTree`, `GameplayStateTree`, and
  `UnrealMCPStateTree`.

Enable the applicable standard engine plugins directly in the target project;
they are dependencies of the authored content and must remain enabled when
those assets need them. The companion descriptors reference those engine
plugins only for the Editor target and do not establish their Game/Shipping
availability. `UnrealMCP` and its companions are editor authoring tools only:
finished assets and Game/Shipping targets must not depend on them. Verify that
every enabled MCP plugin reference in the target `.uproject` is restricted to
the Editor target, for example:

```json
{
  "Name": "UnrealMCPStateTree",
  "Enabled": true,
  "PlatformAllowList": ["Win64", "Mac", "Linux"],
  "TargetAllowList": ["Editor"]
}
```

Apply both allow lists to `UnrealMCP` and `UnrealMCPMetaSound` references. The
platform list also prevents UE 5.6 Plugin Browser enable/disable toggles from
discarding the scoped project reference. The integration project in this
repository declares the base `UnrealMCP` reference as Editor-only and
predeclares both companions disabled and Editor-only. Enable only the companion
needed for the current authoring surface.

When building this checkout from source, run from the repository root:

```powershell
$Project = (Resolve-Path '.\MCPGameProject\MCPGameProject.uproject').ProviderPath
& 'C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Build\BatchFiles\Build.bat' `
  MCPGameProjectEditor Win64 Development -Project="$Project" -WaitMutex
```

Do not run this build while Unreal Editor or Live Coding still owns the target
modules.

### 2. Prepare the external Python server

From the repository root:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python sync --frozen --python 3.11
```

Unreal Editor's embedded Python is separate from this environment. Do not
install the server dependencies into the engine's Python installation.

### 3. Configure the MCP client

Use an absolute script path unless the client is guaranteed to start in the
repository root:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "unrealMCP": {
      "command": "powershell",
      "args": [
        "-NoProfile",
        "-ExecutionPolicy",
        "Bypass",
        "-File",
        "C:\\path\\to\\UnrealMCP5_6_1\\scripts\\run_unreal_mcp_server.ps1"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

Repository-local examples are available in [`mcp.json`](mcp.json) and
[`.mcp.json`](.mcp.json). Those examples use a relative script path and
therefore assume the MCP client starts from this repository root.

The wrapper runs the exact server command used by this checkout:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run --python 3.11 unreal_mcp_server.py
```

### 4. Start Unreal Editor and verify the bridge

Open the project with UnrealMCP enabled. On Windows, check the listener:

```powershell
Test-NetConnection 127.0.0.1 -Port 55561
```

Then restart or reconnect the MCP client. A connected client should initially
see these three tools:

- `list_toolsets`
- `describe_toolset`
- `call_tool`

Use them in that order to discover and invoke domain tools. For example, search
the `niagara` toolset, inspect the exact selected contract, then dispatch it
through `call_tool`. When the returned policy requires confirmation, set the
dispatcher-level `confirm=true` only after reviewing the requested operation.

For a read-only end-to-end bridge check:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_live_bridge.py
```

Exit code `2` means no compatible editor bridge was reachable; it is a skip,
not a successful live test.

## Verified tool catalog

The following snapshot was generated from the registered runtime catalog on
2026-08-27:

| Toolset | Tools | Toolset | Tools |
|---|---:|---|---:|
| AI | 15 | Audio | 4 |
| Batch | 1 | Blueprint | 18 |
| Blueprint Node | 105 | Chaos | 23 |
| Control Rig Authoring | 8 | Data | 9 |
| Editor | 34 | GAS | 6 |
| Ieta | 1 | Landscape | 7 |
| Management | 1 | Material | 21 |
| Networking | 2 | Niagara | 89 |
| Niagara Planning | 8 | PCG | 23 |
| Project | 11 | Python | 1 |
| Sequencer | 10 | Skeletal | 9 |
| StateTree | 5 | Texture Generation | 11 |
| UMG | 7 | **Total** | **429** |

Recalculate the catalog instead of manually guessing after tool changes:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_tool_registration.py
```

Detailed tool documentation starts at
[`Docs/Tools/README.md`](Docs/Tools/README.md). The live MCP schemas returned by
`describe_toolset` remain the authority for current parameters and policy
metadata.

## Discovery and compatibility mode

Search mode is the default and exposes three public schemas while retaining all
429 validated contracts internally. The current checked snapshot reduces the
initial tool schema from 654,607 bytes to 1,296 bytes (99.802%).

An older MCP client can request eager exposure before server startup:

```powershell
$env:UNREAL_MCP_TOOL_EXPOSURE = 'eager'
.\scripts\run_unreal_mcp_server.ps1
```

Eager mode advertises every tool directly and substantially increases the
initial schema payload.

## Safety model

- Treat the MCP connection as editor-privileged access. Tools can create,
  modify, save, move, and delete Unreal assets.
- Commit or shelve work before broad authoring sessions.
- The verified policy catalog contains 99 read, 293 write, 33 destructive, and
  4 lifecycle tools. Fifty-eight contracts require dispatcher confirmation.
- `execute_unreal_python` can run arbitrary Unreal Python with editor
  privileges. Review code before dispatch.
- Prefer dry-run and generated-temp paths when a tool provides them.
- Finished assets should not serialize `/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`,
  `UnrealMCP`, `MCPUnreal`, or `mcp_unreal` references. UnrealMCP is the
  authoring bridge, not a required runtime component of finished content.
- The bridge binds to `127.0.0.1`; do not expose it through a proxy or port
  forward.

See [`Docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md`](Docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md) for connection, build,
version, discovery, and timeout diagnostics.

## Verification

The offline gates below require no running editor:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_tool_registration.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_parity_audit.py --fail-on issues
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_policy_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_discovery_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_exposure_benchmark.py check
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_unreal_bridge_connection.py
```

The complete CI command set is defined in
[`.github/workflows/verify.yml`](.github/workflows/verify.yml).

## Repository layout

```text
MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/  UE 5.6.1 editor bridge plugin
MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCPStateTree/  optional StateTree authoring provider
MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCPMetaSound/  optional MetaSound authoring provider
Python/unreal_mcp_server.py        external stdio MCP server
Python/tools/                      MCP tool registration and wrappers
Python/services/                   bridge and supporting services
Python/scripts/                    offline and live verification scripts
Docs/Tools/                        domain tool documentation
Docs/Analysis/                     generated audits and engineering evidence
scripts/                           repository-level launch and workflow helpers
```

